---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';

import flashcardScreen from '../../../assets/case_study_images/two-sticks/flash-card.png';
import notebookBlank from '../../../assets/case_study_images/two-sticks/notebook-blank.png';
import notebookFilled from '../../../assets/case_study_images/two-sticks/notebook-filled.png';
import searchScreen from '../../../assets/case_study_images/two-sticks/search2.png';
import startScreen from '../../../assets/case_study_images/two-sticks/start1.png';
import writeScreen from '../../../assets/case_study_images/two-sticks/write.png';
import { sitePath } from '../../../config/site';
import BaseDocument from '../../../layouts/BaseDocument.astro';
import '../../../styles/case-study.css';
---

<BaseDocument
  title="Two Sticks case study | Igor Kupchinenko"
  description="How I designed and built Two Sticks, a Telegram prototype for looking up Chinese characters, practising recall, and writing them by hand."
  pageClass="chinesebee-case-study editorial-case-study"
  ogImage={searchScreen.src}
  canonicalPath={sitePath('/case-studies/two-sticks/')}
>
<main id="main-content"><header class="hero"><h1 class="hero-title">Two Sticks</h1><p class="hero__line">Write the character, without leaving the chat.</p><p class="hero__intro">You can recognise a Chinese character and still struggle to write it. I designed and built Two Sticks to connect looking up a character with practising it, all inside Telegram.</p><dl class="facts"><div><dt>Role</dt><dd>Product direction, UX/UI, bot, API, handwriting app</dd></div><div><dt>When</dt><dd>2024, with a diploma project at MPGU</dd></div><div><dt>Research</dt><dd>Based on the diploma team’s research</dd></div><div><dt>Outcome</dt><dd>Shipped prototype, no longer hosted</dd></div></dl><figure class="hero-stage"><div class="hero-stage__phones"><Image src={startScreen} alt="Two Sticks introduction and first learning prompt in Telegram." widths={[240, 360, 480]} sizes="(max-width: 760px) calc((100vw - 60px) / 3), 220px" format="webp" quality={85} loading="eager" decoding="async" fetchpriority="high" /><Image src={searchScreen} alt="Two Sticks word search result with character details and a save action." widths={[240, 360, 480]} sizes="(max-width: 760px) calc((100vw - 60px) / 3), 220px" format="webp" quality={85} loading="eager" decoding="async" /><Image src={writeScreen} alt="Two Sticks handwriting Web App with a drawn character and recognition score." widths={[240, 360, 480]} sizes="(max-width: 760px) calc((100vw - 60px) / 3), 220px" format="webp" quality={85} loading="eager" decoding="async" /></div><figcaption>Finding a character, saving it, and practising handwriting in Telegram.</figcaption></figure></header><section class="chapter" id="context" data-index-section>

  <div class="chapter__body">
    <h2>You recognise it. Can you write it?</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>A dictionary gives you the character, its sound, and its meaning. Reproducing it from memory takes a different kind of practice.</p>
      <p>Printed copybooks make room for that practice, but their exercises are fixed and the work is slow to check. I wanted a learner to be able to find a character, keep it, and practise writing it in the messenger they already used.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="chapter" id="research" data-index-section>

  <div class="chapter__body">
    <h2>Keep the hand involved.</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>The starting point was a 2024 MPGU diploma by Смакова, Шаталова, and Шинкарева, called Two Sticks / Две палочки. Their work covered copybooks, memorisation methods, competing apps and bots, and an open-day masterclass. Fifteen of twenty participants expressed interest in the idea. That was an interest signal, not a usability result for the product I built.</p>
      <p>Their research gave me several ways to help someone practise: cards for recall, character structure and mnemonics for understanding, and copywork for writing. My job was to bring those activities together in a usable product.</p>
      <p>The team’s competitive study included Skritter, Duolingo, HelloChinese, and Telegram bots. Skritter was a reference for dedicated writing practice; the bots offered an easy way in, but focused mostly on vocabulary and commands.</p>
      <p id="direction">I kept search and flashcards in the chat, delivered notebook sheets there, and used a Telegram Web App for handwriting. The words a learner saved would supply the practice, so each lookup could lead somewhere useful.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="chapter chapter--wide" id="journey" data-index-section>

  <div class="chapter__body">
    <h2>A word you save becomes a word you practise.</h2>
    <p class="wide-copy">
      Search starts a personal collection. Flashcards, notebook sheets, and handwriting practice draw on those saved words, without asking the learner to build a separate list for each activity.
    </p>
  </div>

  <ol class="journey-map">
    <li>
      <h3>Find a character. Keep it close.</h3>
      <p class="journey-map__decision">I kept Save on the character card, beside the Chinese, pinyin, meaning, and HSK level. A word found through search could go straight into the learner’s practice collection.</p>
      <figure><Image src={searchScreen} alt="Two Sticks word search result with character details and a save action." widths={[240, 360, 480]} sizes="(max-width: 340px) calc(100vw - 40px), 300px" format="webp" quality={85} loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Save five words. Then practise recall.</h3>
      <p class="journey-map__decision">Flashcards use the learner’s saved words. I made them available once five words were saved, so the questions could offer alternatives. Before then, the empty state tells the learner how many more to add.</p>
      <figure><Image src={flashcardScreen} alt="Two Sticks flashcard practice using the learner’s saved characters." widths={[240, 360, 480]} sizes="(max-width: 340px) calc(100vw - 40px), 300px" format="webp" quality={85} loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure>
    </li>
    <li class="is-sheet">
      <h3>Look at how the character is built.</h3>
      <p class="journey-map__decision">I brought structure, elements, similar characters, a mnemonic, and examples onto one notebook sheet, with a grid for writing. The learner could keep that sheet in the chat and return to it while practising.</p>
      <figure><Image src={notebookFilled} alt="Filled notebook sheet for 教 with structure, stroke order, similar characters, mnemonic, and writing grid." widths={[480, 720, 960]} sizes="(max-width: 660px) calc(100vw - 40px), 620px" format="webp" quality={85} loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Now try writing it.</h3>
      <p class="journey-map__decision">The handwriting app opens inside Telegram with a prompt and a canvas. The learner draws the character, checks it, and can clear it or move to the next word. The score measures recognition similarity to the target character; it does not verify or teach stroke order.</p>
      <figure><Image src={writeScreen} alt="Handwriting Web App with a drawn character, Clear, Check, Next word, and an accuracy score." widths={[240, 360, 480]} sizes="(max-width: 340px) calc(100vw - 40px), 300px" format="webp" quality={85} loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <div class="validation-board">
    <article class="validation-case">
      <p>The prototype is no longer hosted because the bot, API, and Web App require paid infrastructure. The screens and public source code document the working version.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section><section class="chapter chapter--wide chapter--outcome" id="outcome" data-index-section>

  <div class="chapter__body">
    <h2>From a diploma idea to a working prototype.</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>I designed and built the Russian-language Telegram bot, FastAPI backend, PostgreSQL word database, and Vue handwriting app. The implementation used the name Chinese Bee, which remains in the source repositories.</p>
      <p>Alongside the practice shown above, it included facts and a character primer. Live teacher supervision, an HSK statistics dashboard, and plans for growing the audience stayed in the wider diploma brief.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="outcome-board">
    <figure><Image src={notebookBlank} alt="Blank Two Sticks notebook sheet ready for character study." widths={[640, 960, 1280, 1600]} sizes="(max-width: 760px) calc(100vw - 40px), 780px" format="webp" quality={85} loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>A blank notebook sheet for character practice.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure><Image src={writeScreen} alt="Dictation Web App showing handwriting and an accuracy score." widths={[240, 360, 480, 640]} sizes="(max-width: 340px) calc(100vw - 40px), 300px" format="webp" quality={85} loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>Handwriting practice with a recognition score.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
</section><section class="chapter reflection" id="reflection" data-index-section>

  <div class="chapter__body">
    <h2>Does saving a word lead to practising it?</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>The prototype connects lookup and practice. The next question is whether learners make that transition themselves.</p>
      <p>I would look at whether people who save five characters begin handwriting practice, and whether they use the notebook sheets or skip them. That would help me decide where the path needs to be simpler.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section></main><footer class="case-footer"><nav aria-label="Two Sticks project links"><a href="https://github.com/KidPudel/chinesebee_bot">Bot source ↗</a><a href="https://github.com/KidPudel/chinesebee_api">API source ↗</a><a href="https://github.com/KidPudel/chinese-bee-dictation">Dictation source ↗</a><a href={sitePath('/')}>Home</a><a href={sitePath('/case-studies/supergood/')}>SuperGood case study →</a></nav></footer>
</BaseDocument>
